+      <article id="journal-content" class="journal-article">
+        <p>
+          Every exceptional cup of coffee begins long before the green beans ever reach our roastery drum. At The Daily Grind, our bean sourcing philosophy is built on direct-trade partnerships with small-scale, multi-generational farms located along the high-altitude coffee belt. Rather than buying anonymous commodity lots through distant brokers, our sourcing team travels each harvest season to the mist-covered highlands of Yirgacheffe in Ethiopia, the volcanic slopes of Huila in Colombia, and the shaded mountain valleys of Antigua, Guatemala. At elevations between 1,500 and 2,200 meters above sea level, cooler night temperatures slow down the maturation of the coffee cherry, allowing complex sugars, vibrant organic acids, and dense floral aromatics to develop naturally inside every seed.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          During our farm visits, we work side by side with producers to evaluate soil health, shade-canopy biodiversity, and water conservation systems. We exclusively partner with growers who practice selective hand-picking, ensuring only peak-ripeness crimson cherries are harvested while unripe green fruit is left on the branch for later passes. Depending on the regional microclimate and desired flavor profile, the harvested cherries undergo carefully monitored post-harvest processing—ranging from crystal-clear mountain spring washed fermentations that highlight crisp citrus clarity, to raised-bed natural and honey processes that infuse the beans with rich stone-fruit sweetness and velvety body.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Before any lot is selected for shipment, our certified Q-Graders and local farming partners conduct rigorous blind cupping sessions right at origin. We score each micro-lot on fragrance, acidity, mouthfeel, flavor complexity, and aftertaste cleanliness, selecting only specialty-grade coffees that score 86 points or higher. By paying transparent prices that sit at least 40% above Fair Trade minimums—and pre-financing harvests directly with farming cooperatives—we ensure that our partner communities can invest in climate-resilient varietals, wet-mill infrastructure, and fair wages for pickers.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Once the parchment coffee is rested, milled, and vacuum-sealed in moisture-locked GrainPro bags, it journeys to our local roastery. Upon arrival, we run sample roasts on every single sack to map out custom time-and-temperature curves that honor the hard work of the farmer. Whether we are coaxing jasmine and bergamot notes out of an Ethiopian heirloom lot or balancing dark cocoa and toasted hazelnut in a Latin American espresso blend, every batch is roasted to order and cupped daily so you experience the full story of its origin in every sip.
+        </p>
+      </article>
+
